`\n\nI only had the query. I haven't seen the schema, existing indexes, or an `EXPLAIN` plan, so the scans below are what the query forces if the indexes are missing. Check them against the real plan.\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` on the `lower(u.email)` predicate.** A plain index on `email` can't be used when the column is wrapped in a function. The planner has to read every `users` row and lowercase each email. This is the biggest cost, and it grows with the size of `users`.\n\n2. **Full scan of `orders` on the join key `o.user_id`, if that column has no index.** Postgres doesn't index foreign keys automatically, and MySQL/InnoDB only does so when the FK constraint exists. Without an index, each matching user can trigger a scan of `orders`, or the planner falls back to a hash join that reads all of `orders`. The user filter is highly selective, so this is wasteful.\n\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables. This defeats index-only scans and covering indexes, and it carries large columns (text, JSON) across the join and back to the client. It also returns `user_id`/`id` twice, which breaks some drivers that map columns by name.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**Index (Postgres):**\n```sql\n-- Expression index so lower(email) is searchable\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Join key; turns the orders side into an index lookup\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nBoth indexes should give a plan like: index lookup on `users` (1 row), then index lookup on `orders`.\n\n**Rewrite option A: keep the query, select only the needed columns:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\nList the columns the caller actually uses. Lowercasing the literal too keeps the comparison correct if the input casing varies.\n\n**Rewrite option B: avoid the expression index entirely:**\n- Postgres: make the column `citext`, then `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` works with a plain unique index on `email`.\n- MySQL: the default collations are already case-insensitive, so drop `lower()` and index `email` directly. With `lower()` in place, MySQL 8 needs a functional index: `CREATE INDEX idx ON users ((lower(email)));`.\n- Any engine: store emails normalized to lowercase on write, add a unique index on `email`, and query `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'`. This is the cheapest at read time and also prevents duplicate accounts that differ only in case.\n\n## Recommendation\nNormalize emails on write with a unique index on `email` (or use the expression index if you can't change the data). Add the index on `orders.user_id`. Replace `SELECT *` with an explicit column list. If the caller only needs the orders, an `EXISTS`/subquery on `users` avoids carrying user columns at all.\n\nRun `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the `Seq Scan` nodes are gone. If you share the schema and engine, I can tailor the index syntax and check for an existing index.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}